<h2 id="demand">The Physical Demand of Playing Two Ways</h2>
<p>What makes Hunter's role so rare isn't raw testing numbers — it's the conditioning and positional versatility required to play snap counts at two positions that ask for almost opposite skill sets. Wide receiver rewards long-speed and route-running fluidity; cornerback rewards short-area burst, hip flexibility, and the ability to flip and run backward at full speed. Very few players train both skill sets simultaneously at an elite level, which is part of why true two-way NFL players are essentially nonexistent outside of Hunter.</p>
<p>That versatility has shown up in unexpected ways during camp. Hunter even took reps at placekicker during a joint practice with the Carolina Panthers this preseason, connecting on a 28-yard field goal — a lighthearted moment, but also a reminder of how much athletic range he's asked to bring to the roster on a weekly basis. Through camp, Coen has leaned toward using him primarily at cornerback while managing his offensive reps, citing the coaching value of keeping him locked into one side during 11-on-11 work.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>Why Testing Numbers Matter Less Here</strong>
  Most athletic profiles in this series lean on combine numbers because they predict a fairly narrow set of position-specific skills. Hunter's situation is different: no single 40 time or vertical jump captures what it takes to play snap-for-snap at two positions with opposite physical demands. His workload itself — not a stopwatch — is the real test of his athleticism in 2026.
</div>
